Your password has not been changed.

The password you gave is shorter than $_arg_limit characters, and fml
does not store one below that length.

The reason is particular to fml.  Before this version, a password was
stored in a format that kept only its first eight characters and threw
the rest away, so every password an existing list holds is worth eight
characters whatever was typed to set it.  Accepting a new password of
eight or nine would leave you exactly where you started.

Please choose a longer one.  Length is what is being asked for; there is
no requirement to mix letters, digits and symbols, and a longer phrase
you can remember is worth more than a short one you cannot.

  Ten characters or more is the range NISC's handbook treats as safe.
  https://security-portal.cyber.go.jp/handbook/

  NIST SP 800-63B asks for at least fifteen for a password used on its
  own, which an fml administrator password is.
  https://pages.nist.gov/800-63-4/sp800-63b.html
